1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,720
Welcome to this short lesson about Python loops.

2
00:00:02,720 --> 00:00:06,520
By the end, you will understand how a for loop repeats a block of code.

3
00:00:06,520 --> 00:00:08,439
Let us look at a simple for loop.

4
00:00:08,439 --> 00:00:13,400
Notice the structure, the keyword for, the range function, and the body that repeats.

5
00:00:13,400 --> 00:00:17,199
Inside the code editor, you press the run button in the upper right corner.

6
00:00:17,199 --> 00:00:19,480
The program then prints each number below.

7
00:00:19,480 --> 00:00:21,359
Think of the loop as a flow chart.

8
00:00:21,359 --> 00:00:26,600
It starts, checks a condition, runs the body, and repeats until the condition is false.

9
00:00:26,600 --> 00:00:30,560
A while loop is similar, but it checks the condition before each repetition, so it may

10
00:00:30,560 --> 00:00:32,240
run zero times.

11
00:00:32,240 --> 00:00:35,600
This bar chart shows how many times each example loop runs.

12
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:37,719
This is the end of this demo lesson.

13
00:00:37,719 --> 00:00:39,640
Seeing the code on screen adds important detail.
